expanded_sketch:
To prove the main theorem, the paper proceeds by: introducing the functional framework for \begin{equation}\label{eq:main}
    -\Delta u  +V(x)u - \frac{(N-2)^2}{4|x|^2}u =f(x,u), \quad x \in \R^N \setminus \{0\}
\end{equation}
and recalling preliminary results in $X^1(\mathbb{R}^N)$; giving a variational construction of Cerami sequences; establishing a new profile decomposition adapted to the structure of $X^1(\mathbb{R}^N)$; and finally showing the strong convergence of the Cerami sequence and verifying that the limit is a ground state, which completes the proof of the main theorem.

expanded_theorem:
\label{th:main}
Suppose that (V), (F1)--(F4) hold. Then, there exists a ground state solution to
\begin{equation}\label{eq:main}
    -\Delta u  +V(x)u - \frac{(N-2)^2}{4|x|^2}u =f(x,u), \quad x \in \R^N \setminus \{0\}
\end{equation}
.

