ref_dict.thm:existence_mountain_pass:
\begin{theorem}\label{thm:existence_mountain_pass}
Let $K$ and $h$ satisfy \eqref{eq:assumption_H} and assume that the following hypotheses hold:
\begin{itemize}
    \item[\emph{(i)}] There is a point $x_0 \in \partial\D$ with  $\mathfrak{D}(x_0) >  1$;
    \item[\emph{(ii)}] $\partial_\tau \mathfrak{D}(x) \neq 0$ for all $x \in \partial\mathbb{D}$ where $\mathfrak{D}(x) = 1$;
    \item[\emph{(iii)}] $\partial_\nu \Phi(x) < 0$ for all $x \in \partial\mathbb{D}$ where $\partial_\tau \Phi(x) = 0$.
\end{itemize}
Then, problem \eqref{eq:Problem} admits at least one solution.
\end{theorem}

ref_dict.eq:Problem:
\begin{array}{ll}
        -\Delta u = 2K(x)e^{u} & \text{in } \mathbb{D},\\
        \frac{\partial u}{\partial \nu} + 2 = 2h(x)e^{u/2} & \text{on } \partial\mathbb{D}.
    \end{array}\right.
\label{eq:Problem}
\end{equation}
Integrating \eqref{eq:Problem} yields a constraint which corresponds to the Gauss-Bonnet identity:
\begin{equation}
\int_{\mathbb{D}} K(x) e^{u} + \int_{\partial\mathbb{D}} h(x) e^{u/2} = 2\pi.
\label{eq:Gauss-Bonnet}
\end{equation}

Most of the available results in the literature address the problem where at least one of the curvatures to be prescribed is constant (or zero). For instance, the case $h = 0$ was studied in \cite{ChangYang1987} via reflection to a Nirenberg problem on $\mathbb{S}^2$. The zero Gaussian curvature case ($K = 0$) has been extensively investigated, see \cite{ChangLiu1996, DaLioMartinazziRiviere2015,Gehrig2020,  GuoLiu2006,LiLiu2005,LiuHuang2005}. 

When both $K$ and $h$ are nonconstant functions, the problem becomes more intricate, as the interaction of the two curvatures has an effect on the problem that has no counterpart in the classical Nirenberg problem. In \cite{CruzRuiz2018} a new variational approach is developed when both $K$ and $h$ are positive. This variational formulation has been used by Struwe in \cite{Struwe2024} to define a new geometric parabolic flow from which general existence results are derived. On the other hand, in \cite{Ruiz2023} the Leray-Schauder degree of the problem is computed in a compact regime. This last result is based on the blow-up analysis of \cite{JevnikarLopezSorianoMedinaRuiz2022}, that we briefly describe below. Assume that there exists a blow-up sequence of solutions $u_n$ to the problem:
\begin{equation*}
\left\{
    \begin{array}{ll}
        -\Delta u_n = 2K_n(x)e^{u_n} & \text{in } \mathbb{D},\\
        \frac{\partial u_n}{\partial \nu} + 2 = 2h_n(x)e^{{u_n}/2} & \text{on } \partial\mathbb{D},
    \end{array}

expanded_sketch:
To prove the main theorem, the authors use variational methods: solutions to
\begin{array}{ll}
        -\Delta u = 2K(x)e^{u} & \text{in } \mathbb{D},\\
        \frac{\partial u}{\partial \nu} + 2 = 2h(x)e^{u/2} & \text{on } \partial\mathbb{D}.
    \end{array}\right.
\label{eq:Problem}
\end{equation}
Integrating \eqref{eq:Problem} yields a constraint which corresponds to the Gauss-Bonnet identity:
\begin{equation}
\int_{\mathbb{D}} K(x) e^{u} + \int_{\partial\mathbb{D}} h(x) e^{u/2} = 2\pi.
\label{eq:Gauss-Bonnet}
\end{equation}
are found as critical points of the energy functional
\[
\mathcal{I}(u)=\int_{\mathbb{D}}\Big(\tfrac12|\nabla u|^2-2K(x)e^u\Big)+\int_{\partial\mathbb{D}}\Big(2u-4h(x)e^{u/2}\Big).
\]
Since “the geometric properties of $\mathcal{I}$ are far from evident” and “the possibility of blow-up solutions makes the Palais-Smale condition unlikely to hold”, they “first use a perturbation argument”, introducing a one-parameter family $\mathcal{I}_\varepsilon$ with “$\varepsilon$ a small parameter”. The “basic idea” is: “first find critical points of $\mathcal{I}_\varepsilon$ via variational methods, and then pass to the limit to get a solution to the problem above.”

The construction of critical points depends on the sign of $\varepsilon$: for $\varepsilon\ge 0$ the functional is “of mountain-pass type”, whereas for $\varepsilon<0$ they find “a more sophisticated 3-dimensional linking structure”, described using “a comparison argument with the constant coefficient case.”

Because boundedness of (PS) sequences is problematic (“(PS) sequences could eventually diverge in norm”), they use “Struwe’s monotonicity trick” to obtain “bounded (PS) sequences $v_\varepsilon^k$ for almost every $\varepsilon$ small”; letting $k\to\infty$ yields solutions for perturbed problems, “controlling the Morse index of the solutions.” This provides sequences $\varepsilon_n\to 0$ and critical points $u_n$ of $\mathcal{I}_{\varepsilon_n}$ with bounded Morse index.

Next, they use \cite{LopezSorianoMalchiodiRuiz2019} to show “the length and area are uniformly bounded, i.e.,”
\begin{equation} \label{bdedmass} \int_{\mathbb{D}} e^{u_n} + \int_{\partial\mathbb{D}} e^{{u_n}/2} <C.  \end{equation}
For compactness, they argue “by contradiction” adapting blow-up arguments from \cite{JevnikarLopezSorianoMedinaRuiz2022} to the non-geometric perturbed setting, with the perturbation affecting “the estimate of the error terms” and “the resulting asymptotic expansion.” They conclude: if $u_n$ is unbounded, then there is “a unique blow-up point $p\in\partial\mathbb{D}$ with $\partial_\tau\Phi(p)=0$ and $\varepsilon_n\,\partial_\nu\Phi(p)\ge 0$.” This “contradicts either condition $(b^-)$ or $(b^+)$”, hence $u_n$ is bounded and one can “pass to the limit to obtain a solution to the problem above,” completing the proof of the main theorem.

