expanded_sketch:
The authors "outline the proof" as follows. The equation
\begin{align}\label{eq:NDE-spatial}\tag{*}
    -i\gamma^0 \gamma^k\p_k \psi + m\gamma^0\psi -a\psi-\nabla F(\psi)=0.
\end{align}
"admits a variational structure", i.e. it is the Euler--Lagrange equation of an action functional~$J$. However, $J$ "doesn't satisfy the Palais-Smale condition". To remedy this, they "introduce a coercive perturbation multiplied by a small~$\varepsilon\in (0,1]$" and then show the perturbed functional satisfies the Palais--Smale condition, though the $(PS)_c$ bounds "depend on~$\frac{1}{\varepsilon}$". Using "spectral information of the Dirac operator", they show the perturbed functionals admit a "linking structure" with level estimates "uniform in~$\varepsilon$". Even though the linking sets are "both infinite dimensional", they use "the negative gradient flow of the functional together with the Leray-Schauder degree to find a nontrivial linking", yielding "min-max solutions for the perturbed equations". Next, using "the special structure of~$F$ and the perturbation term", they apply "a contradiction argument to get uniform estimates" for perturbed solutions; here "the triviality of the kernel of the geometric Dirac operator on the three-sphere is needed". Finally, these uniform estimates "allow[] to pass along a subsequence to a nontrivial limit" which gives "the desired nontrivial periodic solution".

