(1) "Divergence reformulation: We express $F^{\pm1,\lambda}=\mathrm{div}\,\tilde F^{\pm1,\lambda}$ and establish estimates for $\tilde F^{\pm1,\lambda}$;"
(2) "Integration by parts" to move the divergence onto $b^{\pm1,\lambda}$ in $L^2$ inner products;
(3) "Diffusion cancellation" where the resulting terms are "precisely canceled" using the magnetic diffusion term $\Delta b^{\pm1,\lambda}$.

- For nonzero constant background fields $\mathbf{B_0}\neq\mathbf{0}$, introduce the coordinate transform $x_2\mapsto B_1x_2-B_2x_1$, which "maintains all norm estimates" and gives "exact cancellation of the dominant high-frequency linear term $\mathbf{B_0}\cdot\nabla u^{h,\pm1,\lambda}$" in the $F^{\pm1,\lambda}$ error analysis.

- They also highlight a decay fact used in the estimates: the directional derivative satisfies
\begin{align*}
\|\mathbf{B_0}\cdot\nabla u^{h,\pm1,\lambda}\|_{L^2}\le C\lambda^{-s-\delta}\ll \|u^{h,\pm1,\lambda}\|_{L^2}\le C\lambda^{-s},\quad \lambda\to\infty,
\end{align*}
with "$\delta>0$" giving the improved decay.

- Finally, the stated overall strategy is: "construction of approximate solutions," then "precise estimation of approximation errors," then "rigorous construction of exact solutions," and then "finishing the proof" separately for $\mathbf{B_0}=\mathbf{0}$ and $\mathbf{B_0}\ne\mathbf{0}$.

